X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20261101T200000Z
DTEND:20261101T220000Z
LOCATION:Musical Arts CenterVisit LIVE@jacobs!
SUMMARY:Concert Orchestra and IU Children's Choir – JoAnn Falletta\, gu
 est conductor\; Brent Gault\, chorus master\; Karmen Grubišić\, piano s
 oloist
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy this performance from almost anywhere in the world viaL
 IVE@jacobs!Repertoire  Danny Elfman: Wunderkammer (2022)MacDowell: Piano 
 Concerto No. 2 in D Minor\, Op. 23 (1886)Barber: Symphony No. 1 (in One M
 ovement)\, Op. 9 (1936/43)About the Artists  Multiple Grammy Award-winnin
 g conductor JoAnn Falletta serves as music director of the Buffalo Philha
 rmonic Orchestra (BPO)\, music director laureate of the Virginia Symphony
 \, principal guest conductor of the Brevard Music Center\, and conductor 
 laureate of the Hawaii Symphony. Gramophone Magazine named Falletta as on
 e of the 50 great conductors of all time\, and ASCAP has honored her as "
 a leading force for music of our time." As music director of the BPO\, Fa
 lletta became the first woman to lead a major American orchestra. She has
  guest conducted over 100 orchestras in North America and many of the mos
 t prominent orchestras in Europe\, Asia\, and South America. She is a lea
 ding recording artist for Naxos and has won two individual Grammys\, for 
 Richard Danielpour's The Passion of Yeshua with the BPO and for Kenneth F
 uchs' Spiritualist with the London Symphony Orchestra. Her Naxos recordin
 g of John Corigliano's Mr. Tambourine Man with the BPO received two Gramm
 ys and her Scriabin recording with the BPO was nominated for a 2024 Gramm
 y for Best Orchestral Performance. Falletta is a member of the American A
 cademy of Arts and Sciences\, has served by presidential appointment as a
  member of the National Council on the Arts\, and is the recipient of man
 y of the most prestigious conducting awards. She has conducted over 1\,60
 0 orchestral works by more than 600 composers\,including over 135 works b
 y women composers and over 150 premieres. After completing her bachelor's
  degree at Mannes\, Falletta earned master's and doctoral degrees from Th
 e Juilliard School. When not on the podium\, she enjoys playing classical
  guitar\, writing\, cycling\, and yoga\, and is an avid reader.Brent Gaul
 t is professor of music in music education at the Indiana University Jaco
 bs School of Music. He specializes in elementary and early childhood musi
 c education\, with a focus on active approaches to music learning in chil
 dhood. Gault has served as a presenter and guest lecturer for colleges an
 d music education organizations in the United States\, Brazil\, Canada\, 
 China\, Finland\, Greece\, Ireland\, Scotland\, and Singapore. Articles b
 y Gault have been published in various music education periodicals\, incl
 uding the Bulletin of the Council for Research in Music Education\, the J
 ournal of Research in Music Education\, and Music Educators Journal\, He 
 is coeditor (with Carlos Abril) of General Music: Dimensions of Practice 
 (2022\, Oxford University Press) and Teaching General Music (2016\, Oxfor
 d University Press)\, and author of Listen Up! Fostering Musicianship Thr
 ough Active Listening (2016\, Oxford University Press). In addition to hi
 s duties with the Music Education Department\, Gault serves as the progra
 m director for the Indiana University Children's Choir\, where he conduct
 s the Chamber Choir. He is a past president of the Organization of Americ
 an Kodály Educators. In 2019\, he received the OAKE Outstanding Educator
  Award.  Croatian American pianist Karmen Grubišić began her piano stud
 ies in Boulder\, Colorado\, at age four. Moving to Vienna\, Austria\, she
  studied with Hrvoje Jugović\, and upon returning to the United States\,
  she continued her studies with Andrew Cooperstock\, Robert Hill\, and Ch
 ristopher Harding. Grubišić earned a Bachelor of Music in Piano Perform
 ance at The Glenn Gould School of The Royal Conservatory (GGS) in Toronto
 \, Canada\, under the tutelage of James Anagnoson and Li Wang. She is cur
 rently in her first year of the master's program in piano performance at 
 the Jacobs School of Music\, studying with Norman Krieger. Grubišić has
  won multiple solo\, chamber\, and concerto competitions and has performe
 d as a soloist in the United States\, Canada\, Germany\, Czechia\, and Cr
 oatia. In 2024\, as part of Duo Sciocco\, she won first place in the duos
  category of the GGS Chamber Music Competition. From an early age\, Grubi
 šić has participated in summer music programs and attended festivals ac
 ross the United States and Europe\, including the Brevard Music Center\, 
 Saarburg Festival\, and the Chautauqua Institution. Outside her work as a
  soloist\, Grubišić is an experienced church musician and an avid chamb
 er player. While at the GGS\, she performed in The Royal Conservatory Orc
 hestra as an orchestral keyboardist in several concerts and in the school
 's 2024 and 2025 fall opera productions. Meeting URL: https://liveatjacob
 s.music.indiana.edu/
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Enjoy this performance from almos
 t anywhere in the world via</strong><a href="https://liveatjacobs.music.i
 ndiana.edu/"><strong>LIVE@jacobs</strong></a><strong>!<br /></strong></p>
 <p><strong>Repertoire</strong><br />  Danny Elfman: <em>Wunderkammer (202
 2)<br /></em>MacDowell: <em>Piano Concerto No. 2 in D Minor\, Op. 23 (188
 6)<br /></em>Barber: <em>Symphony No. 1 (in One Movement)\, Op. 9 (1936/4
 3)</em><br /><br /><strong>About the Artists</strong><br /><br />  Multip
 le Grammy Award-winning conductor <strong>JoAnn Falletta</strong> serves 
 as music director of the Buffalo Philharmonic Orchestra (BPO)\, music dir
 ector laureate of the Virginia Symphony\, principal guest conductor of th
 e Brevard Music Center\, and conductor laureate of the Hawaii Symphony. <
 em>Gramophone Magazine</em> named Falletta as one of the 50 great conduct
 ors of all time\, and ASCAP has honored her as "a leading force for music
  of our time." As music director of the BPO\, Falletta became the first w
 oman to lead a major American orchestra. She has guest conducted over 100
  orchestras in North America and many of the most prominent orchestras in
  Europe\, Asia\, and South America. She is a leading recording artist for
  Naxos and has won two individual Grammys\, for Richard Danielpour's <em>
 The Passion of Yeshua</em> with the BPO and for Kenneth Fuchs' <em>Spirit
 ualist</em> with the London Symphony Orchestra. Her Naxos recording of Jo
 hn Corigliano's <em>Mr. Tambourine Man</em> with the BPO received two Gra
 mmys and her Scriabin recording with the BPO was nominated for a 2024 Gra
 mmy for Best Orchestral Performance. Falletta is a member of the American
  Academy of Arts and Sciences\, has served by presidential appointment as
  a member of the National Council on the Arts\, and is the recipient of m
 any of the most prestigious conducting awards. She has conducted over 1\,
 600 orchestral works by more than 600 composers\,including over 135 works
  by women composers and over 150 premieres. After completing her bachelor
 's degree at Mannes\, Falletta earned master's and doctoral degrees from 
 The Juilliard School. When not on the podium\, she enjoys playing classic
 al guitar\, writing\, cycling\, and yoga\, and is an avid reader.<br /><b
 r /><strong>Brent Gault</strong> is professor of music in music education
  at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music. He specializes in elem
 entary and early childhood music education\, with a focus on active appro
 aches to music learning in childhood. Gault has served as a presenter and
  guest lecturer for colleges and music education organizations in the Uni
 ted States\, Brazil\, Canada\, China\, Finland\, Greece\, Ireland\, Scotl
 and\, and Singapore. Articles by Gault have been published in various mus
 ic education periodicals\, including the <em>Bulletin of the Council for 
 Research in Music Education</em>\, the <em>Journal of Research in Music E
 ducation</em>\, and <em>Music Educators Journal</em>\, He is coeditor (wi
 th Carlos Abril) of <em>General Music: Dimensions of Practice</em> (2022\
 , Oxford University Press) and <em>Teaching General Music</em> (2016\, Ox
 ford University Press)\, and author of <em>Listen Up! Fostering Musicians
 hip Through Active Listening</em> (2016\, Oxford University Press). In ad
 dition to his duties with the Music Education Department\, Gault serves a
 s the program director for the Indiana University Children's Choir\, wher
 e he conducts the Chamber Choir. He is a past president of the Organizati
 on of American Kodály Educators. In 2019\, he received the OAKE Outstand
 ing Educator Award.<br /><br />  Croatian American pianist <strong>Karmen
  Grubišić</strong> began her piano studies in Boulder\, Colorado\, at a
 ge four. Moving to Vienna\, Austria\, she studied with Hrvoje Jugović\, 
 and upon returning to the United States\, she continued her studies with 
 Andrew Cooperstock\, Robert Hill\, and Christopher Harding. Grubišić ea
 rned a Bachelor of Music in Piano Performance at The Glenn Gould School o
 f The Royal Conservatory (GGS) in Toronto\, Canada\, under the tutelage o
 f James Anagnoson and Li Wang. She is currently in her first year of the 
 master's program in piano performance at the Jacobs School of Music\, stu
 dying with Norman Krieger. Grubišić has won multiple solo\, chamber\, a
 nd concerto competitions and has performed as a soloist in the United Sta
 tes\, Canada\, Germany\, Czechia\, and Croatia. In 2024\, as part of Duo 
 Sciocco\, she won first place in the duos category of the GGS Chamber Mus
 ic Competition. From an early age\, Grubišić has participated in summer
  music programs and attended festivals across the United States and Europ
 e\, including the Brevard Music Center\, Saarburg Festival\, and the Chau
 tauqua Institution. Outside her work as a soloist\, Grubišić is an expe
 rienced church musician and an avid chamber player. While at the GGS\, sh
 e performed in The Royal Conservatory Orchestra as an orchestral keyboard
 ist in several concerts and in the school's 2024 and 2025 fall opera prod
 uctions.</p> Meeting URL: https://liveatjacobs.music.indiana.edu/
